  "narrative": {
    "northStar": "The Card Catalog",
    "overview": "The interface is a library catalog cabinet. A steel-green drawer front runs across the top with a brass label holder carrying the product name; below it, on a pale cabinet ground, the visitor's question and the answer sit on the left and the catalog cards that answer drew on sit in a rail on the right. Every claim in an answer carries a small numbered mark, and pressing it pulls the matching card out of the rail. The card is where trust is earned: call number in mono, section, the exact sentence highlighted on ruled card stock, a link to the source.\n\nDensity is moderate and reading-first: one typeface family (Atkinson Hyperlegible) at a generous 17px body, hairlines rather than boxes to divide fields, and a single red top rule that identifies card stock at a glance. Light and dark are both first-class. Dark is the cabinet room with the lights down: the ground darkens, but the steel header, the brass and paper plate and the light card stock all stay, so cards keep reading as physical objects. The catalog stays literal and modern; there is no typewriter type and no paper texture.",
    "keyCharacteristics": [
      "Steel drawer-front header with a brass-bordered paper label plate.",
      "Answers on white paper panels; sources on off-white card stock with a 4px red top rule.",
      "Numbered ink marks in the answer that pull a card (slide left, lift, focus).",
      "One family: Atkinson Hyperlegible for all text, its Mono only for call numbers, card marks and code.",
      "Both schemes keep the materials; only the ground and the text set on it change."
    ],
    "rules": [
      {
        "name": "The Two Inks Rule",
        "body": "Text on the ground uses ground-ink tokens; text on card stock or paper uses ink tokens. In dark mode these diverge, so never set ink directly on the ground or ground-ink on stock.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Blue Means Go Rule",
        "body": "Blue appears only on links and focus rings. Accent color for emphasis comes from red (source identity) or brass (holders and labels on steel).",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Lights Down Rule",
        "body": "Dark mode darkens the ground only. Steel, brass, paper and card stock stay light enough that ink text on them is unchanged.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Mono Is A Stamp Rule",
        "body": "Mono is for identifiers only: call numbers, card marks, model names and question IDs. Never for prose or headings.",
        "section": "typography"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Label Names A Set Rule",
        "body": "Uppercase labels are headings for a group of things (a drawer, a rail of cards, a table column). They never sit above a larger headline as an eyebrow.",
        "section": "typography"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Only Cards Lift Rule",
        "body": "Shadow belongs to catalog cards. Answer panels, drawer groups, chips and fields stay flat with a 1px hairline.",
